<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu al-Abbas Ahmad II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1370">1370</a>–<a href="/years/1394">1394</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu al-Abbas Ahmad II was a Hafsid sultan who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> from 1370 to 1394. Already ruler of Constantine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Bejaia</a>, he took Tunis in 1370 and spent his reign reuniting the dynasty's lands, subduing Arab tribes and rebellious towns in the south. He withstood a French and Genoese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdia">Mahdia</a> in 1390 and restored Hafsid power, a revival continued by his son Abu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fari">Faris</a>.</p></div>
